      "math_academy_id": "q-340871",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Let $F(x)=-2{x}^{3}+3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
